\f1\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Silence rose like a dank mist. The occasional sound of attendees pouring water and gulping, and clicking pens, only defined the edges of the silence more sharply and made it deeper.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Anyone?\'92 Gerry asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, it\'92s \'85 I mean \'85\'92 the speaker coughed and slaked more water.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There\'92s been a development \'85 since we first reconnoitred the site in the Pale, a development application was approved. Apparently a religious memorial \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry threw his hands up. He knew where this was going: an Airborne\'92s idea of philanthropy for the Cloudborne almost exclusively to dedicate a statue or park or monument, to remind the Cloudborne of the Airborne\'92s virtuous superiority over them. There were only two kinds of Airborne works below the Layer: building foundations for works above, or \'91Holy Art\'92 which glorified their principles of meritocracy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Which Saint this time?\'92 Gerry asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Saint Zucker.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Tax!\'92 Gerry cursed\'97the patron saint of privacy protection and identity mining, one of the nine major saints of Airborne canon. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s not just a statue.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It never is.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91This one, allegedly, will be the Great Viral Cleansing Memorial and Museum.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91At least that will be what it looks like. It will, of course, be a front for something else.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, can we delay construction until after we\'92ve been over the site?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s too late for that. The statues\'92s almost finished.\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What. It\'92s only \'85\'92 Gerry grasped for a sense of time amid his panic. \'91\'85 six weeks since we cased the site. How could \'85?\'92 But Gerry knew how fast any Airborne could act in matters of sanctimonious aggrandisement.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry knew how these things worked. The statue is the first thing to be erected, often hastily. Once the Trojan Statue has as little as two big toes, subsequent phases of the project are set in stone\'97no one can oppose the construction of a building associated with a statue of a saint, especially one of the Nine.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Amid the solidifying quiet, Gerry heard the feint tapping of fingers a keyboard. A keyboard? Down the far end of the table, one the minions typed furiously, illuminated by the unfashionable glow of an oversized screen clearly built for function rather than status. She raised her hand, tentatively. Everyone else at the table stared at her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes?\'92 Gerry barked. \'91What is it?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I think I might have a solution.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><\'85>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The minion typed a long sequence on her keyboard, and a holographic map of the city appeared on the table in front of her. After what seemed like enough typing to produce paragraphs of text, the map enlarged, moved to the centre of the table and spread out over its surface. More excessive keystroking produced a three-dimensional pin over the map. \'91Here\'92s where the bioweapons bunker is,\'92 the minion said while her fingers never stopped racing of the keyboard. \'91And here, less than two blocks away \'85\'92 She waited for the next pin to appear. \'91\'85 is the site of the March nightclub bombing.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What about it?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With more clacks of keys, the minion zoomed in on the bomb site. As you can see, demolition is almost complete. It\'92s literally a big hole in the ground.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What happening there in the street next to the hole?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, I think the nightclub that was in the building is still operating.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Out in the street?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Many of those seated at the table, incredulous, shook their heads. More than one snorted dismissively and uttered, \'91Huh. Cloudborne. What do you expect?\'92 There were many nods and grimaces of agreement.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The minion kept tapping on her keyboard. Gerry laughed as he thought she was conversing with her computer like a pair of ants. Gerry thought he was funny. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Another map rose up out of the first. \'91This shows underground structures around the same depth we expect the bunker to be,\'92 the minion said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91So what?\'92 blustered one of the attendees. who was becoming impatient with someone junior getting so much attention at the tab\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry saw it. \'91The sub-basement of the bomb site is about the same depth as the bunker.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Quite right, sir,\'92 the minion said, and Gerry had to control himself to keep from beaming from the praise. Instead, he looked momentarily embarrassed. \'91There are a few structures in between the two sites\'97pipes and conduits, and the like.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We could could dig across from the bomb site to the target site,\'92 Gerry said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Correct, sir. That\'92s the idea.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91My idea, That\'92s my idea.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The minion swallowed, as more senior folks at table looked on in horror, unsure if the minion was smart enough to agree.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, sir. And might I say? \'85 thinking so laterally and, in fact, literally laterally, is evidence of true genius, of true leadership.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Everyone relaxed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Might I also table some observations. I mean, I now a true leader would already have it sorted, but if I may be allowed to explain for the rest of the group \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, indeed. Go ahead.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91As you can see, there are the usual tangle of foundations and utility tunnels, but if we track this way under this street, we\'92ll encounter little more than the occasional sewer pipe. And most of what we\'92ll be digging through is clay, which rather conveniently rests on a layer of sandstone. The good news is, it could be quicker to dig across from the bomb site than straight down, as we\'92d originally planned.\'92
